{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzeigqZLzkfwVJ9ED3kjbu","type":"tool_result","content":"===\n3540060 claude -p You are iteration 1 of a Ralph loop in the repository at /home/al/dev/MOSP. You have a hard wall-clock cap of 4 hours for this session; plan the work so that the report section and PROGRESS.md are written well before that. If the full study would not fit, sample and say so in the report.  Read these two files first, in full:  --- TASK.md --- # TASK: A one-parameter theory of MOSP in the excess, on random and industrial instances  ## Philosophy - **Read first, in full**:   - `reports/excess_phase_plan.md`, the plan this loop executes: the     parameter, what stands, the questions, and the three places the record     corrects the brief that produced it.   - `CLAUDE.md`: the owner's rules on compute (only unsolved problems; never     the Python pathwidth solver; never `solutions_dir=None`), what is proved     and what is measured, and \"Lessons that cost sessions\".   - `reports/random_graph_first_moment.md` (Theorems A, A′, A″, B on     `G(n, c/n)`), `reports/scratch/clique_cover_moment/README.md` and     `REPORT_generator.md` (the clique-cover first moment, the fitted     generator, the threshold `e > 1`), `paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex`     (how the paper states it now), and `reports/ml_nature.md` §25, §36, §37     (the ridge as excess; what it is a transition of).   - The Lean headers: `lean/MOSPFormalization/EmptyPair.lean`,     `FirstMoment.lean`, `FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean`,     `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`, `TreeBound.lean`, `Sandwich.lean`     (`branch_lemma`), `IndependenceBound.lean`. - **The parameter.** `e = (n_ones − m) / n`: mean patterns per customer   minus one; `r (c − 1)` for `r = m / n`, `c` customers per pattern; and   `e − 1 + κ / n` is the cyclomatic number per customer of the incidence   graph (`κ` its components). Use realised `e` from the matrix, never the   nominal `r (d − 1)`: the ge