{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgbBWiBCQfGoP9X4EYwVd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 01 is done, the gate passes, and nothing is left to request.\n\n**Outcome.** The map exists: `learning/excess.py` places all 64,291 instances we hold by their excess, with every regenerated ensemble and simulation matrix matching its recorded ones count, and `reports/excess_phase_diagram.md` §1 holds the findings, every table in full, the figure and the regenerate commands. Eight new tests pass. PROGRESS.md and iterations.md are updated to 1 of 13 solved. Nothing was solved or re-solved and `solutions/` was not touched.\n\n**What the map says.**\n- **The corpus has no forest regime.** Zero corpus instances sit below the giant-component line, 12 sit between it and 1, and exactly 2 of 6,376 are incidence forests. A forest theorem would describe nothing we hold directly.\n- **Industry sits between the threshold and the ridge; the benchmark generators do not.** SCOOP has a median excess of 1.35 with 19 of 24 instances in [1, 2), and 10 of 24 decompose. Harvey, Simonis, Shaw, Wilson and Challenge are 80 to 100 percent above 2.4.\n- **The ridge lies between the Chu & Stuckey classes.** With equal customers and patterns, density 2 sits at 1.48 to 2.02 and density 4 at 2.83 to 3.73. The paper's Appendix E sentence placing the hardest classes on the ridge still needs the correction item 12 owns.\n- **The bound-gap instances are the sparse ones at their size.** The median gap instance's excess sits at the 7th percentile of its size cell. That is §6's \"connected and sparse\" in one parameter.\n- **The stated check is not met.** The largest component of the incidence graph reaches one half at excess 0.83 to 0.93 on the five observable series, which is 0.21 to 0.43 above the predicted line, outside the ±0.15 tolerance. The criterion could not have been met: at the branching-factor-one point the giant fraction is zero by definition. So the line is not to be used later as a half-point. What the data show it does separate is \"mostly forests\" below it from \"a few cycles\" above it, and excess 1 separates \"a few cycles\" from none.\n\n**Two pre-existing gate faults fixed, outside the item's paths.** Both were already failing in the first gate run and neither touches a Lean proof. The test pin in `learning/sandwich.py` still expected the §24 conjecture to carry a sorry after it was proved on 2026-10-31, so I promoted it and updated the inventory test. The file `Search/WrongOptimumData.lean` had no line in the root import file, so I added one. The third gate run passed every stage.\n\n**Next** is item 02, the optimum over customers against the excess. The excess-matched series it needs already exist and every cell's realised excess is in `learning/data/excess.csv`, so no regeneration is needed there."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":651,"cache_read_input_tokens":200493,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":736,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fbbd79f-1a23-452c-9cee-0dd980004c76","uuid":"527c97aa-48bb-4c2e-b7d0-c0c70066dc1e","timestamp":"2026-10-31T23:28:08.922Z","request_id":"req_011CgbBWhpsAChnPs9diEoLW"}
